summary:
json.load() fails, the message says Expecting value: line 1 column 1 (char 0) , and the file looks fine in your editor. So you paste it into a validator, it passes, and you're stuck. The fix is to stop guessing. A JSONDecodeError tells you exactly which character the parser choked on ( e.pos ), and the message itself names the cause. Here are six inputs that break json.load() , the exact error each one produces on Python 3.13, and a 12-line helper that shows you the bad spot. Six broken inputs, six different messages import json , codecs , pathlib p = pathlib . Path p ( "empty.json" ) . write_text ( "" ) p ( "bom.json" ) . write_bytes ( codecs . BOM_UTF8 + b '{"name": "Asha"}' ) p ( "single.json" ) . write_text ( "{'name': 'Asha'}" ) p ( "trailing.json" ) . write_text ( '{"name": "Asha", "city": "Udaipur",}' ) p ( "jsonl.json" ) . write_text ( '{"id": 1}
{"id": 2}
' ) for f in [ "empty.json" , "bom.json" , "single.json" , "trailing.json" , "jsonl.json" ] : try : with open ( f , encoding = "utf-8" ) as fh : json . load ( fh ) except json . JSONDecodeError as e : print ( f , "->" , e ) Output on Python 3.13.5: empty.json -> Expecting value: line 1 column 1 (char 0) bom.json -> Unexpected UTF-8 BOM (decode using utf-8-sig): line 1 column 1 (char 0) single.json -> Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes: line 1 column 2 (char 1) trailing.json -> Illegal trailing comma before end of object: line 1 column 35 (char 34) jsonl.json -> Extra data: line 2 column 1 (char 10) Read the message, not just the char number. Each one points to a different fix. 1. Expecting value ... (char 0) : there is no JSON at all char 0 means the parser found nothing it could start with. The usual causes: The file is empty. Something truncated it, or you opened it with "w" before reading. It's an HTML page. This is the big one with APIs. requests.get(url).json() on a 404, a login redirect, or a Cloudflare page gives you <!DOCTYPE html> , and json.loads("<!DOCTYPE html>") fails with the exact same char 0 message. Only whitespace. json.loads(" 
") fails at line 2 column 1 (char 4) , which is the end of the string. For API calls, check the response before parsing: r = requests . get ( url , timeout = 10 ) r . raise_for_status ( ) if "application/json" not in r . headers . get ( "Content-Type" , "" ) : raise ValueError ( f "Expected JSON, got {r.headers.get('Content-Type')}: {r.text[:200]!r}" ) data = r . json ( ) 2. Unexpected UTF-8 BOM : the file was saved by Windows tooling Notepad, Excel exports and some PowerShell commands put three invisible bytes ( EF BB BF ) at the start. Python even tells you the fix in the message: with open ( "bom.json" , encoding = "utf-8-sig" ) as fh : data = json . load ( fh ) # {'name': 'Asha'} utf-8-sig strips the BOM if it's there and behaves like plain UTF-8 if it isn't, so it's safe to use everywhere you read files you didn't write yourself. A related trap: PowerShell 5's > redirect writes UTF-16 . Opening that with encoding="utf-8" doesn't even reach the JSON parser. It dies with UnicodeDecodeError: 'utf-8' codec can't decode byte 0xff in position 0 . Reading the file as bytes and passing them to json.loads() works, because json auto-detects UTF-8, UTF-16 and UTF-32 from raw bytes: data = json . loads ( open ( "u16.json" , "rb" ) . read ( ) ) 3. Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes : it's a Python dict, not JSON {'name': 'Asha'} is what print(my_dict) or str(my_dict) produces. JSON needs double quotes. Fix the writer, not the reader: use json.dump(obj, f) instead of f.write(str(obj)) . If you're stuck with old files that were written that way, ast.literal_eval() can read them safely. Don't use eval() . 4. Illegal trailing comma : hand-edited config JSON doesn't allow a comma after the last item. Older Python versions report this as Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes , which is confusing. Python 3.13 names it directly. The position ( char 34 ) lands right on the stray comma. 5. Extra data : it's JSON Lines, not one JSON document The first object parsed fine, and then the parser found more at line 2. That's a .jsonl / NDJSON file (one object per line), common in logs and data exports. Parse it line by line: with open ( "jsonl.json" , encoding = "utf-8" ) as fh : rows = [ json . loads ( line ) for line in fh if line . strip ( ) ] # [{'id': 1}, {'id': 2}] 6. The one that doesn't fail: NaN json.loads('{"score": NaN}') happily returns {'score': nan} . Python accepts it, but strict parsers (JavaScript's JSON.parse , most APIs) reject it. If you produce JSON for someone else, use json.dumps(obj, allow_nan=False) . It raises ValueError: Out of range float values are not JSON compliant: nan instead of quietly shipping invalid JSON. A 12-line helper that shows the bad character When the file is big, line and column numbers aren't enough. Print the bytes around e.pos : import json def load_json_debug ( path ) : raw = open ( path , "rb" ) . read ( ) print ( f "{len(raw)} bytes, starts with {raw[:4]!r}" ) text = raw . decode ( "utf-8-sig" ) try : return json . loads ( text ) except json . JSONDecodeError as e : lo = max ( e . pos - 30 , 0 ) print ( f "{e.msg} at line {e.lineno}, col {e.colno}" ) print ( repr ( text [ lo : e . pos + 30 ] ) ) print ( " " * ( e . pos - lo + 1 ) + "^" ) raise On the trailing-comma file it prints: 36 bytes , starts with b ' { "na' Illegal trailing comma before end of object at line 1, col 35 'me" : "Asha" , "city" : "Udaipur" , } ' ^ The first line alone catches most cases. 0 bytes means an empty file, b'\xef\xbb\xbf{' means a BOM, b'\xff\xfe{\x00' means UTF-16, and b'<!DO' means HTML. Quick checklist Read the message : "Expecting value" vs "BOM" vs "property name" vs "Extra data" are different bugs. Look at the first 4 bytes of the file or response. For APIs, check the status code and Content-Type before calling .json() . Read files you didn't write with encoding="utf-8-sig" . Write JSON with json.dump() , never str(dict) . If you want to see where a large document breaks before writing code, paste it into a JSON formatter and validator . It points at the failing line. For the basics of json.load vs json.loads , nested data and writing files back, there's a longer guide on reading JSON files in Python , and a roundup of other common Python errors and how to fix them. I write practical debugging guides at Blogs World .
